     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg per day. That is about twice the maximum labeled metformin dose of 2,550 mg per day (usually 2,000 mg per day for the immediate-release form). A dose that high raises the risk of lactic acidosis, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nAlso, you've said you're not a clinician. Medication orders normally need to come from a licensed prescriber, who would be responsible for the dose.\n\nHere are some ways to proceed:\n- **If there's a typo:** Tell me the intended dose. For example, 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg per day) or 1,000 mg twice daily would both be within the limit.\n- **If a prescriber wants this dose:** Have them confirm it directly. I can enter it with a note recording that they confirmed the dose on purpose.\n- **If you're unsure:** Ask the treating physician or a pharmacist to review the order."
      }
     ],
